[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I inspected the OSPF virtual link the “clever people” referred to and found it healthy. The component in question is the OSPF virtual link (VLINK0) that stitches the backbone across non‑backbone Area 1. On r4 and r8, the OSPF process shows a full virtual adjacency: r4 reports neighbor 18.1.4.1 Full on VLINK0 and r8 reports neighbor 14.1.4.1 Full on VLINK0. Their configurations explicitly define the same virtual link endpoints (“area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1” on r4 and “area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1” on r8), and both routers’ OSPF summaries show “Number of full virtual adjacencies going through this area: 1,” confirming the VL is up. Downstream effects also look correct: core routers (e.g., r3) are learning Area 2/Area 1 routes via the VL, and end‑to‑end reachability between the “far areas” is intact—pc0 can ping pc12 with 0% loss.

Mechanism: if this VL were down, the backbone would appear partitioned and routes from the far side (behind r8) would “fall off the map.” Currently, the VL is operational, so the routing domain is contiguous.

Localization: OSPF VLINK0 between r4 (RID 14.1.4.1) and r8 (RID 18.1.4.1).

Root cause: No current fault—OSPF virtual link is established and functioning as designed.

Corrective fix: No changes required. If the earlier outage coincided with a device restart, ensure FRR/OSPF is enabled to start on boot on r4 and r8 and consider monitoring the VLINK0 adjacency so any transient drops are alerted immediately. === END ===